---
import Term from "../Term.astro";
import "./sternzeit.css";
---

<figure class="moon-calendar" id="moon-calendar">
    {/* The container the month buttons size by; not the figure, as a container scopes the counter that numbers it. */}
    <div class="calendar">
    <div class="calendar-head">
        {/* Up to three months either side, as many as the width has room for. */}
        {[-3, -2, -1].map((offset) => <button type="button" data-month={offset} class={`calendar-month-${-offset}`}></button>)}
        <button type="button" class="calendar-title" data-field="title" aria-pressed="true"></button>
        {[1, 2, 3].map((offset) => <button type="button" data-month={offset} class={`calendar-month-${offset}`}></button>)}
    </div>
    <div class="calendar-weekdays" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="calendar-weeks" data-field="weeks"></div>
    </div>
    {/* One hatching for every disc below the horizon, referenced from each day's own SVG. */}
    <svg width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" style="position: absolute">
        <defs>
            <pattern id="moon-calendar-hatch" width="2" height="2" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)">
                <line x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="2" class="calendar-hatch-line" />
            </pattern>
        </defs>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        A month of Moons at the chosen place, each day at the chosen time of day on the chosen clock, as seen there:
        the phase, the night side in <Term of="Earthshine">earthshine</Term>, the Moon's equator and prime meridian
        shifted by the <Term of="Libration">libration</Term>, and all of it turned towards the zenith by the{" "}
        <Term of="Parallactic angle">parallactic angle</Term>. Beside the date, where it stands then: its compass point and, after the arrow, its altitude above the horizon. Hatched, it is below the horizon. Under each
        week, from midnight to midnight, the Moon above the horizon (top lane, yellow while low, where the long path
        through the air reddens it) and the Sun (bottom lane, fading out through civil twilight); the ticks mark the
        chosen time. Hover a day for its moonrise, moonset and highest point, click it to
        make it the page's moment, with Ctrl (Cmd on a Mac) to turn to its month too.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./mooncalendar.js";
</script>
